<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis-Nicolas Davout</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1770">1770</a>–<a href="/years/1823">1823</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Marshal Davout</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis-Nicolas Davout (1770–1823) was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s ablest marshals, victor over the Prussians at Auerstedt in 1806 and created Duke of Auerstedt and Prince of Eckmühl. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 1813–1814 he held the city against a Russian-led siege, expelling thousands of inhabitants and seizing the funds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburger-bank">Hamburger Bank</a>. He surrendered it only after Napoleon's abdication.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout.jpg" alt="Louis-Nicolas Davout" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Pierre-Claude Gautherot / Tito Marzocchi de Bellucci · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_nicolas_davout.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis-Nicolas d'Avout was born on 10 May 1770 at Annoux in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, the eldest son of an impoverished minor noble family with a tradition of military service. He was educated at the military academy at Brienne-le-Château, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> also attended, and at the École Militaire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and in 1788 became a sous-lieutenant of cavalry. He embraced the Revolution, but was removed from the active list because of his noble birth. He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> campaign of 1796 and accompanied General Louis Desaix on Napoleon Bonaparte's Egyptian expedition. Napoleon promoted him to general of division and arranged his marriage to Aimée Leclerc, sister-in-law of Napoleon's sister Pauline.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Davout was one of the original 18 Marshals of the Empire in 1804, the youngest and least experienced of them, which earned him the hostility of other generals. At Austerlitz his III Corps bore the brunt of the allied attack after a forced march of 48 hours. At Auerstädt in 1806 his single corps of 28,000 men defeated the main Prussian army of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, which had more than 63,000, and the battle gave him the nickname Iron Marshal. He became governor-general of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1807, Duke of Auerstädt, and after Wagram Prince of Eckmühl. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812 he commanded the I Corps and led the rear guard in the retreat until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> replaced him with Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-ney">Michel Ney</a>.</p><p>In 1813 Davout took command at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, which he held through a long siege, surrendering only on the order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s abdication. He expelled up to 25,000 of the city's poorest citizens into the winter, and many died of cold and starvation. He was a stern disciplinarian who forbade plundering on pain of death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the Hundred Days Davout was Minister of War, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> kept him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> rather than give him a field command. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> he directed the defence of Paris until the provisional government ordered him to negotiate. He was exiled and deprived of his titles, but they were restored in 1817 and he became a Peer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1819. He died in Paris on 1 June 1823 and lies in the Père Lachaise Cemetery. Contemporaries counted him among the ablest of Napoleon's marshals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis-Nicolas Davout” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French troops occupied the city in 1806, and in 1813–1814 Marshal <b>Louis-Nicolas Davout</b> held it against a Russian-led siege, expelling thousands of poorer inhabitants in midwinter.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Annoux, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.63,4.05&amp;year=1804" aria-label="Annoux on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4645 495 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis-Nicolas Davout"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5095 765h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5095 765h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.63,4.05&amp;year=1804">What was here in 1804? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
